        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1066,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the inverse of a square matrix: the matrix that undoes it. If a matrix turns, stretches or shears points, its inverse turns, stretches or shears them back; the two multiplied together give the identity (1s down the diagonal, 0s elsewhere), which changes nothing.\n\nUse it to go back from a transformed space to the original one: a camera's view back to the room, a body's frame back to the world, a calibration undone.\n\nA matrix that flattens space (its determinant is 0: two of its rows point the same way) cannot be undone, and the node sends nothing. t.linalg.pinv gives the closest thing.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.inv\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA square matrix, or a stack of them (each is inverted). Every input sends.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ninverse:\nThe inverse, the same shape.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe matrix 2 1, 1 1 and its inverse 1 -1, -1 2. Multiplied together (right) they give the identity, 1 0, 0 1.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.inv is the numpy version. t.linalg.solve solves A x = b directly, which is quicker and more accurate than multiplying by the inverse. t.linalg.det says whether a matrix can be inverted. t.linalg.pinv works for any matrix.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1050,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the inverse of a square matrix: the matrix that undoes it. If a matrix turns, stretches or shears points, its inverse turns, stretches or shears them back; the two multiplied together give the identity (1s down the diagonal, 0s elsewhere), which changes nothing.\n\nUse it to go back from a transformed space to the original one: a camera's view back to the room, a body's frame back to the world, a calibration undone.\n\nA matrix that flattens space (its determinant is 0: two of its rows point the same way) cannot be undone, and the node sends nothing. t.linalg.pinv gives the closest thing.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.inv\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA square matrix, or a stack of them (each is inverted). Every input sends.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ninverse:\nThe inverse, the same shape.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe matrix 2 1, 1 1 and its inverse 1 -1, -1 2. Multiplied together (right) they give the identity, 1 0, 0 1.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.inv is the numpy version. t.linalg.solve solves A x = b directly, which is quicker and more accurate than multiplying by the inverse. t.linalg.det says whether a matrix can be inverted. t.linalg.pinv works for any matrix."
        },
